ages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:146:\t// the Solid gate forbids sharing one between them, so the cost is paid twice by\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:147:\t// construction rather than by choice.\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:148:\tS5: { physicalLoc: 73, structuralNodes: 343 },\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:149:\t// S6 likewise has no handwritten reference, so this is a budget. It is the\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:150:\t// corpus's cheapest scenario per DOM site: whitespace-sensitive text costs\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:151:\t// nothing structural at all - the observable is which characters end up\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:152:\t// between the nodes, not how many nodes there are.\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:153:\tS6: { physicalLoc: 73, structuralNodes: 353 },\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:154:\t// S7 likewise has no handwritten reference, so this is a budget. It is the\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:155:\t// corpus's most EXPENSIVE scenario on physical lines and the second most on\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:156:\t// structural nodes: four control types, three `checked` bindings, six\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:157:\t// `attribute`-kind bindings and four computed derivations on one host. The\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:158:\t// two numbers pull apart here - more lines than S2 for fewer nodes - because\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:159:\t// most of S7's cost is per-attribute rather than per-element, and the\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:160:\t// formatter gives every attribute its own line once a tag carries three.\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:161:\tS7: { physicalLoc: 118, structuralNodes: 573 },\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:162:\t// S9 likewise has no handwritten reference, so this is a budget. MEASURED off\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:163:\t// the emitted output, not predicted from S7's. It carries SEVEN dynamic\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:164:\t// bindings across five hosts plus two dynamic texts and still costs FEWER\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:165:\t// physical lines than S6, which is the reading that matters for the axis it\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:166:\t// exists to prove: a boolean content attribute lowered to `kind: 'property'`\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:167:\t// costs the React emitter exactly what any other prop costs — `disabled={locked}`\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:168:\t// is the same shape as the `data-stage={stage}` printed beside it in the same\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:169:\t// start tag — so the repair T049 shipped is FREE in this lane, and the number\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:170:\t// is what says so rather than a comment claiming it.\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:171:\t// S8 likewise has no handwritten reference, so this is a budget, MEASURED off\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:172:\t// the emitted output. It is the CHEAPEST scenario in the corpus on both axes -\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:173:\t// fewer lines than S1 - and that is the reading the async axis needs: an\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:174:\t// authored `async` handler with an `await` inside it costs this lane the same\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:175:\t// shape a synchronous one costs. What the number does NOT show, and this is\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:176:\t// the point of recording it, is the 12.2 repair: `setPhase` is called TWICE\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:177:\t// here, once either side of the boundary, and `setTicks` is a functional\npackages/frameworks/react/test/size.test.ts:178:\t// updater rather than a closure read. Both